    <tr><td><strong>Luxury homes</strong></td><td>★★★★★</td><td>Book-matched marble + brass + black is the current top-end request in our order book</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Compact / low-light homes</strong></td><td>★★★</td><td>Strictly light-dominant or pale tonal; dark-heavy schemes need daylight the room doesn't have</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Rental homes</strong></td><td>★★★★</td><td>The most renter-friendly style — paint, textiles and black accessories do 80% of it reversibly</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Farmhouse</strong></td><td>★★</td><td>Fights the setting; warm rustic palettes serve it better</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p><strong>Room sizes:</strong> small rooms take the pale tonal treatment and gain size; large rooms and open plans can afford dark drama and use tone shifts (pale living flowing into a charcoal media nook) to zone without walls — a trick monochrome does better than any partition.</p>
<h3>The buyer profile, honestly drawn</h3>
<p>The style clusters at 25–45: design-aware professionals, creatives and photographers (who literally see in this palette), bachelors and DINK couples wanting maximum look per rupee, and luxury buyers at the book-matched end. NRIs like its resale safety. <strong>Retired couples</strong> suit the warm tonal mode; the graphic mode's contrast can feel harsh for all-day living. <strong>Families with kids:</strong> tonal greige with performance fabrics works well; white sofas and gloss black in a toddler home is a cleaning subscription — we say so upfront. <strong>Pet owners:</strong> mid-grey everything; fur vanishes on the bridge tones and glows on both extremes. <strong>Who should skip it:</strong> lovers of warm traditional color, festival-decorators whose marigold and rangoli seasons deserve a palette that welcomes them, and anyone who felt a small loss reading "a red cushion is a leak."</p>
